# Experiment 5: providers block embedded web views for sign-in (Google doc, read 2026-11-07)
Source page (fetched): https://developers.google.com/identity/protocols/oauth2/native-app
Verified wording, taken from the page text itself (curl + text extraction of the
`disallowed_useragent` error entry):

  "disallowed_useragent — The authorization endpoint is displayed inside an
   embedded user-agent disallowed by Google's OAuth 2.0 Policies. iOS and macOS
   developers may encounter this error when opening authorization requests in
   WKWebView. Developers should instead use iOS libraries such as Google
   Sign-In for iOS or AppAuth, ..."

  Also from the same page: "Developers should allow general links to open in the
  default link handler of the operating system, which includes both Universal
  Links handlers or the default browser app. The SFSafariViewController library
  is also a supported option." — SFSafariViewController is allowed because it
  shares the browser's cookie store; WKWebView is not.

Credential-exposure reason (RFC 8252 section 8.12, quoted from the fetched
https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8252.txt): embedded user-agents have "the host
app being able to copy user credentials and cookies as well as the user needing
to authenticate from scratch in each app"; RFC 8252 also notes embedded
user-agents let "good and bad actors alike" use them, so malicious apps are
harder to detect.

Conclusion: nsh-auth must never carry a provider's authorize page in a
`webview` (nsh capability `webview`) — Google rejects it outright with
disallowed_useragent, Facebook's manual flow expects a controlled webview only
for its legacy desktop path, and any embedded browser hands the user's password
and cookies to the embedding app. Launchers use the system browser surface:
ASWebAuthenticationSession (iOS/macOS), Custom Tabs (Android), the default
browser + loopback (desktop), redirect/popup (web).
